('127.262.d.4.A',4,'A group with a need but no money to buy is not yet a:','["Segment", "Niche", "Target", "Real market"]'::jsonb,3,'Needs buying power.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.B',3,'A target market is the group a business:','["Chooses to serve with its marketing efforts", "Cannot find", "Avoids", "Owns"]'::jsonb,0,'Defined audience.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.B',4,'A toy company designing a toy for toddlers will target:','["Teenagers only", "Parents and caregivers of young children", "Retirees only", "Lawyers only"]'::jsonb,1,'Buyers are caregivers.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.C',3,'Niche marketing focuses on:','["The whole population", "No one", "A small, specialized segment", "All industries"]'::jsonb,2,'Specialization.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.C',4,'A store selling only left-handed tools is an example of:','["Mass marketing", "Wholesale", "Franchising", "Niche marketing"]'::jsonb,3,'Narrow group.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.D',3,'Analyzing a target market in the fitness industry could include studying members'' age and:','["Exercise habits", "Shoe color", "Name length", "Eye color"]'::jsonb,0,'Relevant traits.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.D',4,'A new running shoe brand would find its target market by examining:','["Who rides buses", "Who runs and what they value", "Who sells cars", "Who sleeps late"]'::jsonb,1,'Study relevant behavior.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.E',3,'Selling machinery to factories is an example of which market?','["Government only", "Business-to-consumer", "Business-to-business", "Consumer-to-consumer"]'::jsonb,2,'Customers are businesses.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.E',4,'A bakery selling bread to shoppers is in which market?','["Wholesale only", "Business-to-business", "Government only", "Business-to-consumer"]'::jsonb,3,'Customers are individuals.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.F',3,'An effective market is one where a business clearly:','["Understands and serves customer needs", "Avoids sales", "Ignores customers", "Hides prices"]'::jsonb,0,'Customer focus.','original'),
('127.262.d.4.F',4,'A coffee shop that succeeds near a college likely does so because it:','["Has no menu", "Matches its offerings to student habits", "Is far away", "Closes at noon"]'::jsonb,1,'Fit the market.','original'),
('127.262.d.5.A',3,'Market segmentation means dividing a market into:','["Random parts", "Equal land parcels", "Groups with similar needs or traits", "Single people only"]'::jsonb,2,'Segments share traits.','original'),
('127.262.d.5.A',4,'The main purpose of segmentation is to:','["Raise taxes", "Reduce all costs", "Avoid customers", "Focus marketing on groups most likely to buy"]'::jsonb,3,'Better targeting.','original'),
('127.262.d.5.B',3,'Segmenting by age, income, and gender is:','["Demographic segmentation", "Behavioral segmentation", "Geographic segmentation", "Psychographic segmentation"]'::jsonb,0,'Demographics.','original'),
('127.262.d.5.B',4,'Segmenting by lifestyle, values, and interests is:','["Geographic segmentation", "Psychographic segmentation", "Usage segmentation", "Demographic segmentation"]'::jsonb,1,'Psychographics.','original'),
('127.262.d.5.C',3,'Culture affects buying decisions through shared:','["Bus routes", "Phone numbers", "Values, beliefs, and customs", "Shoe sizes"]'::jsonb,2,'Culture shapes choices.','original'),
('127.262.d.5.C',4,'A holiday-based custom that boosts demand for a food item is an example of:','["Random chance", "Price discount", "Poor timing", "Cultural influence on buying"]'::jsonb,3,'Customs drive demand.','original'),
('127.262.d.5.D',3,'A clothing store creating separate ads for teens and for adults applies:','["Market segmentation", "Overstocking", "Mass marketing", "Price fixing"]'::jsonb,0,'Different messages for segments.','original'),
('127.262.d.5.D',4,'A bank offering student accounts and retirement accounts is segmenting by:','["Hair length", "Life stage", "Eye color", "Zip code only"]'::jsonb,1,'Stages have different needs.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.A',3,'Marketing information helps managers to:','["Avoid planning", "Predict the lottery", "Make better decisions about customers and products", "Print money"]'::jsonb,2,'Information reduces risk.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.A',4,'A company decides to extend store hours after reviewing customer traffic data. This shows information:','["Being lost", "Being hidden", "Being ignored", "Influencing a decision"]'::jsonb,3,'Data drives action.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.B',3,'A survey given directly to customers collects:','["Primary data", "Old data", "Secondary data", "No data"]'::jsonb,0,'Self-collected data.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.B',4,'A tool for gathering primary data from small groups of customers is a:','["Annual report", "Focus group", "Census report", "Tax table"]'::jsonb,1,'Groups discuss.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.C',3,'Secondary data differ from primary data because they were:','["Never collected", "Made up", "Collected earlier by someone else", "Collected only by you"]'::jsonb,2,'Existing data.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.C',4,'A key advantage of secondary data is that they are usually:','["Always new", "Always exact", "Collected for your question", "Faster and cheaper to obtain"]'::jsonb,3,'Already available.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.D',3,'Analytics is the process of:','["Examining data to find useful patterns", "Counting pens", "Hiding data", "Making coffee"]'::jsonb,0,'Data analysis.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.D',4,'A store reviewing which promotions led to repeat visits is using:','["Packaging", "Analytics", "Inventory", "Guessing"]'::jsonb,1,'Pattern-finding.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.E',3,'Sources of data a business can analyze include sales records, website traffic, and:','["Fortune cookies", "Tides", "Customer surveys", "Moon phases"]'::jsonb,2,'Real data sources.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.E',4,'Loyalty card purchase histories are a source of:','["Property data", "Weather data", "Tax data", "Customer buying behavior data"]'::jsonb,3,'They show purchases.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.F',3,'A key business metric that shows how much each new customer costs to acquire is:','["Customer acquisition cost", "Phone minutes", "Trip length", "Gross weight"]'::jsonb,0,'Cost per new customer.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.F',4,'If a firm spends $5,000 on ads and gains 100 new customers, the acquisition cost per customer is:','["$5", "$50", "$500", "$0.02"]'::jsonb,1,'5,000/100 = 50.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.G',3,'After analyzing data, a business should make:','["Random changes", "No changes", "Recommendations for improving operations", "Secret changes"]'::jsonb,2,'Analysis ends in action.','original'),
('127.262.d.6.G',4,'If data show most online carts are abandoned at checkout, a sound recommendation is to:','["Raise prices", "Add more steps", "Remove products", "Simplify the checkout process"]'::jsonb,3,'Fix the friction.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.A',3,'Businesses decide prices by considering costs, competition, and:','["What customers will pay", "The color of the logo", "Parking space", "Employee hobbies"]'::jsonb,0,'Demand matters.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.A',4,'A firm that sets its price after finding the cost per unit and adding a percentage uses:','["Loss-leader pricing", "Cost-plus pricing", "Auction pricing", "Competitive pricing"]'::jsonb,1,'Markup method.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.B',3,'Common goals of pricing include profit, market share, and:','["Delay", "Confusion", "Survival or image", "Waste"]'::jsonb,2,'Goals vary.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.B',4,'A new firm that sets low prices to win many customers quickly has a goal of:','["Avoiding customers", "Raising prices later only", "Reducing sales", "Gaining market share"]'::jsonb,3,'Share-seeking.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.C',3,'Factors affecting price include supply and demand, perceived value, and:','["Competitors'' prices", "Month names", "Names of employees", "Color of boxes"]'::jsonb,0,'Competition matters.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.C',4,'If customers see a product as very valuable, a business can usually charge:','["A random price", "A higher price", "A lower price", "No price"]'::jsonb,1,'Perceived value raises price.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.D',3,'The break-even point is where total revenue equals:','["Taxes", "Profit", "Total cost", "Zero"]'::jsonb,2,'No profit or loss.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.D',4,'Fixed costs of $6,000, a $15 price, and $9 variable cost give a break-even of:','["6,000 units", "400 units", "666 units", "1,000 units"]'::jsonb,3,'6,000/6 = 1,000.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.E',3,'Pricing a product at $9.99 rather than $10.00 is called:','["Odd pricing", "Loss leading", "Prestige pricing", "Even pricing"]'::jsonb,0,'Odd ending.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.E',4,'A loss leader is a product priced:','["Above everything", "Below cost to attract customers", "At random", "Never"]'::jsonb,1,'Draws traffic.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.F',3,'When supply of a product rises and demand stays the same, price tends to:','["Rise", "Double", "Fall", "Stay locked"]'::jsonb,2,'More supply lowers price.','original'),
('127.262.d.7.F',4,'When demand for a product grows and supply stays the same, price tends to:','["Fall", "Disappear", "Stay the same always", "Rise"]'::jsonb,3,'More demand raises price.','original'),
('127.262.d.8.A',3,'A product line is a group of related products, and a product mix is:','["All the lines a company sells", "The price list", "Only the cheapest", "One item"]'::jsonb,0,'Mix contains lines.','original'),
('127.262.d.8.A',4,'The number of different product lines a firm offers is called the mix''s:','["Depth", "Width", "Height", "Length"]'::jsonb,1,'Width counts lines.','original'),
('127.262.d.8.B',3,'New product ideas are important because they help a business:','["Lower sales", "Lose customers", "Stay competitive and grow", "Cut staff only"]'::jsonb,2,'Innovation keeps firms current.','original')
